Title:
DutchOven — Application-scoped Windows network brownouts for deterministic red-team validation

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
DutchOven is a small native C tool that creates deterministic, time-bounded “brownouts” by intermittently blocking new outbound connections for specific executables on Windows. It solves the problem of reliably validating agent/app retry logic, buffering, health transitions, and recovery behavior without stopping services or applying permanent firewall rules. The implementation uses Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) application-ID scoped filters on ALE_AUTH_CONNECT_V4/V6, toggled on a block/pass duty cycle with atomic IPv4+IPv6 transactions. It runs in a dynamic WFP session so filters and the generated sublayer are automatically cleaned up when the session closes, even on interruption. The repo also provides a Cobalt Strike Beacon Object File (BOF) variant for short inline “pulses” and an integration harness with a TCP canary to prove impact and cleanup. It’s useful primarily for red teams/pentesters doing controlled resilience testing of EDR/agents/proxies and for defenders validating client behavior under partial network degradation.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Filtering Platform (WFP) dynamic sessions, sublayers, and filter lifecycle
- App-ID (executable path) scoped filtering at ALE_AUTH_CONNECT_V4/V6
- Deterministic block/pass scheduling (“brownout” duty cycles) and bounded runtime
- Atomic add/remove of IPv4 and IPv6 filters (transactional changes)
- BOF implementation details: COFF, dynamic function resolution, minimal imports/no CRT
- Validation methodology: canary traffic, baseline/control comparisons, residue checks

Use Cases:
- Validate EDR/agent reconnect and telemetry buffering behavior under intermittent egress loss
- Test application retry/backoff logic without killing processes or services
- Create repeatable network-degradation experiments for incident response drills or QA
- Inline “pulse” gating from Cobalt Strike Beacon to quickly disrupt a target app’s new connections
- Evidence-driven testing with JSONL output and integration harness to confirm no WFP residue

Keywords:
Windows Filtering Platform, WFP, Base Filtering Engine, Fwpuclnt.dll, ALE_AUTH_CONNECT_V4, ALE_AUTH_CONNECT_V6, application ID, executable path filtering, dynamic WFP session, sublayer, firewall filters, outbound connect authorization, IPv4, IPv6, transactional filter updates, Cobalt Strike, Beacon Object File, BOF, COFF, dynamic function resolution, JSON Lines